12:["$","section",null,{"className":"reference-section battle-flow-section","id":"battle-flow","children":["$","$L1e",null,{"guide":{"date":"September 16–17, 1862","place":"Sharpsburg, Maryland, from the upper crossings of Antietam Creek and the northern battlefield around the Poffenberger farm, East Woods, Miller cornfield, and Hagerstown Turnpike to McClellan’s headquarters at the Pry farm east of the creek","result":"McClellan arranged a main attack against Lee’s left, an incompletely defined movement by Burnside against Lee’s right, and a conditional attack from the United States center. On the afternoon of September 16, Hooker’s I Corps crossed the upper Antietam, moved west, turned south, and met Hood’s Confederates in the East Woods. Mansfield’s XII Corps crossed later to support Hooker. The opposing troops remained close through a rainy night before the general battle opened at dawn on September 17","facts":[{"label":"Episode scope","value":"McClellan’s plan and the final movements before Antietam","note":"The episode examines how the Army of the Potomac was arranged, then follows Hooker’s I Corps across the upper Antietam into the evening skirmish with Hood on September 16."},{"label":"Documentary problem","value":"No written general order set out the battle plan on September 16","note":"McClellan did not call a conference of corps commanders. The episode therefore reconstructs intent from verbal directions and descriptions written after the battle."},{"label":"Preliminary report","value":"Main attack on Lee’s left; movement against his right; conditional center attack","note":"McClellan’s October 15 report said the center would advance after one or both flank movements were “fully successful,” using whatever reserve remained."},{"label":"Final report","value":"A more definite sequence written and published later","note":"The final report described Hooker and Mansfield attacking first, Burnside moving when the northern action looked favorable, and the center advancing after either flank succeeded. The episode treats this later formulation cautiously because it was written with knowledge of the battle’s course."},{"label":"Clearest element of the plan","value":"Hooker would open against the Confederate left","note":"McClellan’s reports, Hooker’s official report, and the actual movement on September 16 all support this conclusion."},{"label":"Burnside’s assignment","value":"Attack Lee’s right, but without a specified start time","note":"McClellan also directed Burnside to protect the army’s southern flank against a possible Confederate arrival from Harpers Ferry. Burnside understood his role as less than an immediate all-out assault."},{"label":"Center attack","value":"Dependent on success at one or both flanks","note":"The plan did not define how much success would trigger this commitment, leaving McClellan to decide during the battle whether and when to use the center offensively."},{"label":"Episode’s interpretive frame","value":"McClellan planned against a force he believed nearly equal","note":"The hosts argue that fear of defeat shaped his dispositions more than a determination to destroy Lee. That is the podcast’s interpretation; the force estimates and deployment are separately documented."},{"label":"McClellan’s final-report figures","value":"97,445 Confederates against 87,164 United States troops","note":"The episode rounds these to 97,400 and 87,160 and emphasizes that neither represented troops actually available on the battlefield."},{"label":"McClellan’s likely working estimate","value":"Roughly 85,000 Confederates against 87,000 United States troops","note":"The episode derives this from an earlier 100,000 estimate reduced by a reported 15,000 Confederate casualties at South Mountain. It is an explanation of McClellan’s perception, not an accepted strength count."},{"label":"United States strength in the episode","value":"About 72,500 for September 17","note":"The U.S. Army Center of Military History gives approximately 72,700 effectives. The small difference reflects rounding and counting conventions."},{"label":"Confederate strength at the opening","value":"About 31,000–36,000 in the episode’s estimate","note":"McClellan had about 60,000 immediately available, with roughly 12,500 men of VI Corps marching from Pleasant Valley. Strength figures vary by time and by whether present, effective, or engaged personnel are counted."},{"label":"Wing system abandoned","value":"McClellan resumed direct control of the corps","note":"The campaign’s temporary wings under Franklin, Sumner, and Burnside were dissolved on the eve of battle. Hooker left Burnside’s wing, and XII Corps left Sumner’s control."},{"label":"Command consequence","value":"Burnside retained the outlook of a wing commander without the wing","note":"The episode argues that the unexplained reorganization and Burnside’s resentment contributed to command friction on the United States left on September 17."},{"label":"Northern striking force","value":"I and XII Corps under Hooker’s sector command","note":"The episode estimates the combined force at about 20,000; National Park Service interpretation places approximately 18,000 I and XII Corps soldiers in the northern bivouacs. Both figures are approximate."},{"label":"Southern force","value":"About 13,000 in IX Corps","note":"Burnside’s corps deployed opposite the lower bridge and Confederate right. Jacob D. Cox exercised tactical command of the corps while Burnside retained overall direction."},{"label":"United States center","value":"II Corps, two V Corps divisions, and Pleasonton’s cavalry","note":"II Corps was the army’s largest corps at nearly 18,000. V Corps and cavalry backed it, and VI Corps would join the reserve after arriving from Pleasant Valley."},{"label":"Troops held near the center","value":"More than 45,000 under McClellan’s immediate control","note":"The figure includes formations assigned to the center and reserve rather than a single attack column. The episode reads this concentration as both a possible offensive mass and insurance against failure on either flank."},{"label":"Cavalry placement","value":"About 4,300 horsemen massed in the center","note":"The episode argues that keeping Pleasonton’s cavalry there limited reconnaissance and suggests McClellan may have envisioned exploiting a breakthrough in the center."},{"label":"Artillery reserve","value":"Heavy guns on high ground east of Antietam Creek","note":"Twenty-pounder Parrott rifles south of the middle bridge could reach much of the battlefield while remaining difficult for Confederate field artillery to answer."},{"label":"Headquarters","value":"The Philip Pry farm","note":"McClellan directed the battle from the high ground between the upper and middle crossings. The position commanded a broad view but was more than a mile from much of the northern fighting."},{"label":"Hooker’s order","value":"Cross the upper Antietam and turn Lee’s left","note":"McClellan issued the order at about 2 p.m. on September 16. The crossing began around 3:30–4 p.m."},{"label":"Crossing points","value":"Upper Bridge and Pry’s Ford","note":"Meade’s Pennsylvania Reserves and Ricketts’s division used the bridge; Doubleday’s division crossed at the ford nearby."},{"label":"Route after crossing","value":"West toward the Hagerstown Turnpike, then south toward Sharpsburg","note":"Hooker’s divisions advanced in parallel columns before turning to feel for the Confederate left. The northern route was not well reconnoitered beforehand."},{"label":"Hooker’s command experience","value":"Eleven days in command of I Corps","note":"Promoted to corps command on September 6, Hooker had led the formation at South Mountain but remained unfamiliar with a corps inherited from Pope’s Army of Virginia."},{"label":"Request for support","value":"Hooker warned that one corps alone was exposed","note":"After crossing, he asked for reinforcement. McClellan sent Mansfield’s XII Corps, which crossed late and bivouacked about a mile behind Hooker."},{"label":"Confederate warning chain","value":"Cavalry observers to Stuart, then Stuart to Lee","note":"The report reached Lee while he met with Jackson and Longstreet in Sharpsburg. Lee responded by sending Hood north and moving Jackson’s divisions behind and to Hood’s left."},{"label":"Evening contact","value":"Meade’s Pennsylvanians met Hood in the East Woods","note":"Infantry and artillery fought near dusk on September 16. The main clash subsided with darkness, but opposing pickets exchanged intermittent fire during the night."},{"label":"Night before battle","value":"Rain, no secure rest, and opposing lines within hearing distance","note":"Hooker reported visiting pickets close enough to hear movement in the Confederate line. Soldiers wrote letters and diary entries while expecting a general battle at dawn."},{"label":"Senior leader casualties in scope","value":"None killed or mortally wounded during the September 16 probe","note":"The episode ends before the major fighting of September 17. It introduces leaders whose later battlefield fates belong to subsequent episodes."},{"label":"Recommended book","value":"“To Antietam Creek” by D. Scott Hartwig","note":"The episode recommends the first volume of Hartwig’s Maryland Campaign study, which carries the narrative through the eve of Antietam."},{"label":"Transcript normalizations","value":"Sumner, Pleasonton, Ricketts, Parrott rifles, Pry House, Pry’s Ford, and Ezra A. Carman","note":"The automated transcript produces forms including “Smurse,” “Pleasanton,” “Rickett,” “parrot rifles,” “pry house,” “Prize Ford,” and “Ezra Carmen.” This guide uses standard historical spellings."}],"maps":[{"title":"McClellan’s intended sequence and initial deployment","caption":"Hooker and Mansfield formed the northern striking force, Burnside’s IX Corps faced Lee’s right at the lower bridge, and more than 45,000 men remained in or near the United States center and reserve. McClellan’s preliminary report made the center attack dependent on flank success without defining the threshold. Unit footprints and routes are generalized. This map is schematic and not to scale.","source":"McClellan’s official reports, U.S. Army Center of Military History, Antietam order of battle, and episode transcript; see sources below.","image":"/images/ep189-mcclellan-battle-plan.svg","alt":"Schematic map of McClellan’s Antietam plan showing Hooker and Mansfield attacking Lee’s left, Burnside facing Lee’s right, and Sumner, Porter, Pleasonton, and Franklin concentrated near the United States center and reserve."},{"title":"Hooker crosses the upper Antietam","caption":"On September 16, Meade and Ricketts crossed the Upper Bridge while Doubleday used Pry’s Ford. I Corps moved west, turned south, and met Hood near the East Woods around dusk. Mansfield followed later and bivouacked behind Hooker. Routes and unit footprints are generalized. This map is schematic and not to scale.","source":"Hooker’s and Meade’s official reports, Antietam National Battlefield interpretation, Antietam Battlefield Board tablets, U.S. Army Center of Military History, and episode transcript; see sources below.","image":"/images/ep189-hooker-september-16-advance.svg","alt":"Schematic northern Antietam map showing Meade and Ricketts crossing at the Upper Bridge, Doubleday crossing at Pry’s Ford, I Corps turning south toward Hood in the East Woods, and Mansfield following near midnight."},{"title":"Hooker’s headquarters on the northern battlefield","caption":"Alexander Gardner photographed the house identified by the Library of Congress as Hooker’s headquarters at Antietam. National Park Service interpretation places Hooker’s September 16 headquarters in the Poffenberger barn; the photograph records the farm landscape where I Corps bivouacked before its dawn attack.","source":"Library of Congress Prints and Photographs Division and Antietam National Battlefield; see sources below.","image":"/images/ep189-hooker-headquarters.jpg","alt":"Alexander Gardner photograph of the Poffenberger farm landscape identified as General Joseph Hooker’s headquarters at Antietam."}],"moments":[{"time":"1:10","audioTime":70,"title":"The eve of Antietam is recapped","detail":"McClellan has passed up attacks on September 15 and 16, but Lee remains at Sharpsburg and a general engagement can no longer be postponed."},{"time":"2:25","audioTime":145,"title":"Delay enlarges the possible prize","detail":"By waiting, McClellan inadvertently allows more of Lee’s divided army to assemble where a Federal victory might destroy it."},{"time":"3:52","audioTime":232,"title":"McClellan begins laying the groundwork","detail":"The episode immediately qualifies that description: he does not establish the Confederate dispositions or give detailed written orders to his corps commanders."},{"time":"4:25","audioTime":265,"title":"The plan remains difficult to reconstruct","detail":"Sparse communication on September 16 forces historians to infer McClellan’s intentions from later reports and the movements actually made."},{"time":"6:39","audioTime":399,"title":"Two postbattle versions are introduced","detail":"McClellan did not provide a general written description until after Antietam, and his preliminary and final reports differ."},{"time":"6:48","audioTime":408,"title":"The preliminary plan is quoted","detail":"It calls for a main attack on Lee’s left, an effort against his right, and a center attack after one or both flank movements become fully successful."},{"time":"7:15","audioTime":435,"title":"The later report modifies the sequence","detail":"McClellan’s final report makes Hooker and Mansfield the opening force and delays Burnside until matters look favorable in the north."},{"time":"8:37","audioTime":517,"title":"The right hook is the clearest element","detail":"The episode finds firm evidence for the blow against Lee’s northern flank but greater uncertainty over Burnside’s exact mission and timing."},{"time":"9:09","audioTime":549,"title":"Success is left undefined","detail":"McClellan does not state what degree of progress on the flanks would cause him to release the center and reserve."},{"time":"10:23","audioTime":623,"title":"Lee’s position is still vulnerable","detail":"Despite the plan’s ambiguity, Lee’s weak center, exposed flanks, limited reserve, and single principal retreat route leave the Confederates in danger."},{"time":"11:13","audioTime":673,"title":"Army organization creates another problem","detail":"The temporary three-wing structure of the Maryland Campaign is reviewed before McClellan’s abrupt return to direct corps control."},{"time":"11:50","audioTime":710,"title":"Hooker receives the spearhead role","detail":"I Corps will lead the northern attack with Mansfield’s newly commanded XII Corps in support."},{"time":"12:15","audioTime":735,"title":"The wing system is dissolved","detail":"Hooker leaves Burnside’s control and XII Corps leaves Sumner’s, producing frustration and unresolved command friction on the eve of battle."},{"time":"13:03","audioTime":783,"title":"McClellan’s reported numbers are tested","detail":"His final report claims more than 97,000 Confederates against about 87,000 Federals, figures the episode contrasts with battlefield strength."},{"time":"14:50","audioTime":890,"title":"Actual United States strength is estimated","detail":"The episode gives approximately 72,500 for the Army of the Potomac on September 17."},{"time":"15:58","audioTime":958,"title":"Hooker and Mansfield form the northern force","detail":"I Corps crosses first, and XII Corps is added after Hooker warns that a lone corps would be exposed on the northern battlefield."},{"time":"16:32","audioTime":992,"title":"The rest of the army is placed","detail":"IX Corps faces the lower bridge, II Corps occupies the center, and V Corps, cavalry, and arriving VI Corps elements form the center and reserve."},{"time":"18:37","audioTime":1117,"title":"The intended attack sequence is summarized","detail":"Hooker attacks first, Burnside moves against the southern end, and the center becomes available either to exploit success or reinforce a repulse."},{"time":"19:26","audioTime":1166,"title":"The center is treated as the critical sector","detail":"The concentration could support an eventual breakthrough or serve as the army’s last defensive line if a flank attack failed."},{"time":"21:38","audioTime":1298,"title":"Federal heavy guns dominate from the east bank","detail":"The artillery reserve’s twenty-pounder Parrott rifles can reach much of the field from high ground south of the middle bridge."},{"time":"23:41","audioTime":1421,"title":"Geography limits the apparent flexibility","detail":"A corps in the center would need roughly ninety minutes to reach Hooker by the upper crossing, while a direct move across the middle bridge meant attacking Lee’s center."},{"time":"24:45","audioTime":1485,"title":"Burnside receives an imprecise order","detail":"He is told to attack Lee’s right the next morning but receives no start time and must also guard against a possible Confederate threat from Harpers Ferry."},{"time":"26:41","audioTime":1601,"title":"The Pry House becomes McClellan’s command post","detail":"The headquarters offers a broad view from east of Antietam Creek but separates McClellan from rapidly changing conditions on the flanks."},{"time":"28:41","audioTime":1721,"title":"Hooker is ordered across","detail":"At about 2 p.m. on September 16, McClellan directs I Corps to use the Upper Bridge and nearby Pry’s Ford and feel for Lee’s left."},{"time":"29:04","audioTime":1744,"title":"The divisions use two crossings","detail":"Meade and Ricketts take the bridge while Doubleday crosses at the ford, then the three columns move west and turn south."},{"time":"31:24","audioTime":1884,"title":"Hooker asks for reinforcement","detail":"He warns that the Confederates will overwhelm his lone corps, prompting McClellan to send Mansfield across behind him."},{"time":"31:50","audioTime":1910,"title":"Confederate cavalry detects the movement","detail":"The warning passes through Stuart to Lee, who sends Hood north and orders Jackson’s divisions into supporting positions."},{"time":"32:39","audioTime":1959,"title":"Meade and Hood clash in the East Woods","detail":"A sharp infantry and artillery skirmish develops as daylight fades, then dwindles to scattered fire in the darkness."},{"time":"33:20","audioTime":2000,"title":"Hooker promises to attack at dawn","detail":"I Corps holds its advanced ground while Mansfield’s supporting corps settles behind it during a steady rain."},{"time":"33:58","audioTime":2038,"title":"Sebastian Duncan records the soldiers’ mood","detail":"The XII Corps lieutenant’s diary entry asks for courage to do his duty and accepts that the coming battle may be fatal."},{"time":"34:34","audioTime":2074,"title":"The book recommendation closes the episode","detail":"The hosts recommend D. Scott Hartwig’s “To Antietam Creek: The Maryland Campaign of September 1862.”"}],"command":[{"side":"United States","color":"#315f8c","branches":[{"commander":"Maj. Gen. George B. McClellan","command":"Army of the Potomac","subordinates":"Directed the army from the Pry farm; assigned the opening attack to Hooker, an attack against the Confederate right to Burnside, and retained the center and reserve under his immediate control"},{"commander":"Maj. Gen. Joseph Hooker","command":"I Corps and northern sector","subordinates":"Led the opening movement with the divisions of Abner Doubleday, James B. Ricketts, and George G. Meade; Mansfield’s XII Corps was ordered to support him"},{"commander":"Brig. Gen. George G. Meade","command":"Pennsylvania Reserves Division, I Corps","subordinates":"Crossed the Upper Bridge in the lead and made the principal evening contact with Hood near the East Woods"},{"commander":"Brig. Gen. James B. Ricketts","command":"2nd Division, I Corps","subordinates":"Crossed at the Upper Bridge and formed on Hooker’s left in supporting distance"},{"commander":"Brig. Gen. Abner Doubleday","command":"1st Division, I Corps","subordinates":"Crossed at Pry’s Ford and formed on Hooker’s right as the corps turned south"},{"commander":"Maj. Gen. Joseph K. F. Mansfield","command":"XII Corps","subordinates":"Led Alpheus S. Williams’s and George S. Greene’s divisions across the upper Antietam late on September 16 to support Hooker"},{"commander":"Maj. Gen. Edwin V. Sumner","command":"II Corps","subordinates":"Commanded the largest United States corps, held in the center east of Antietam Creek until ordered into battle the next morning"},{"commander":"Maj. Gen. Fitz John Porter","command":"V Corps","subordinates":"Held two divisions near the center and reserve and served as one of McClellan’s closest battlefield advisers"},{"commander":"Maj. Gen. Ambrose E. Burnside","command":"IX Corps direction on the United States left","subordinates":"Received verbal instructions to attack the Confederate right while also guarding against a possible threat from Harpers Ferry; Jacob D. Cox exercised tactical corps command"},{"commander":"Maj. Gen. William B. Franklin","command":"VI Corps","subordinates":"Marched two divisions from Pleasant Valley to join Porter and the army reserve on September 17"},{"commander":"Brig. Gen. Alfred Pleasonton","command":"Cavalry Division","subordinates":"Commanded approximately 4,300 cavalrymen concentrated near the army center; the 3rd Pennsylvania Cavalry accompanied Hooker’s advance"}]},{"side":"Confederacy","color":"#9a3d32","branches":[{"commander":"Gen. Robert E. Lee","command":"Army of Northern Virginia","subordinates":"Received warning of Hooker’s crossing while meeting Jackson and Longstreet in Sharpsburg and reinforced the threatened left"},{"commander":"Maj. Gen. Thomas J. “Stonewall” Jackson","command":"Left Wing","subordinates":"Shifted his divisions behind and to the left of Hood to meet the United States movement on the northern battlefield"},{"commander":"Maj. Gen. James Longstreet","command":"Right Wing","subordinates":"Sent Hood’s two brigades north from their reserve near the Dunker Church when Lee learned of Hooker’s advance"},{"commander":"Brig. Gen. John Bell Hood","command":"Hood’s Division, Longstreet’s Wing","subordinates":"Moved the Texas and Law brigades toward the East Woods and checked Meade’s evening advance"},{"commander":"Maj. Gen. J. E. B. Stuart","command":"Cavalry Division","subordinates":"Relayed cavalry reports of the United States crossing from the northern battlefield to Lee in Sharpsburg"}]}],"people":[{"name":"George B. McClellan","role":"Army of the Potomac commander whose verbal orders and later reports provide the surviving outline of the Antietam plan","side":"United States"},{"name":"Joseph Hooker","role":"I Corps commander assigned to cross the upper Antietam and open the attack against Lee’s left","side":"United States","image":"/images/ep189-joseph-hooker.jpg","source":"Library of Congress, Brady-Handy photograph collection"},{"name":"Joseph K. F. Mansfield","role":"New XII Corps commander ordered to reinforce Hooker after the I Corps crossing","side":"United States"},{"name":"Ambrose E. Burnside","role":"Former wing commander directing IX Corps on the United States left opposite the lower bridge","side":"United States"},{"name":"Jacob D. Cox","role":"United States general exercising tactical command of IX Corps under Burnside’s direction","side":"United States"},{"name":"Edwin V. Sumner","role":"Senior corps commander whose nearly 18,000-man II Corps began the battle in the center","side":"United States"},{"name":"Fitz John Porter","role":"V Corps commander holding two divisions near McClellan’s center and reserve","side":"United States"},{"name":"William B. Franklin","role":"VI Corps commander marching from Pleasant Valley to join the reserve","side":"United States"},{"name":"Alfred Pleasonton","role":"Army of the Potomac cavalry chief whose division was concentrated in the center","side":"United States"},{"name":"George G. Meade","role":"Pennsylvania Reserves commander whose division led Hooker’s advance and met Hood in the East Woods","side":"United States"},{"name":"James B. Ricketts","role":"I Corps division commander who crossed the Upper Bridge and formed on Hooker’s left","side":"United States"},{"name":"Abner Doubleday","role":"I Corps division commander who crossed Pry’s Ford and formed on Hooker’s right","side":"United States"},{"name":"Alpheus S. Williams","role":"XII Corps division commander who had led the corps temporarily before Mansfield’s arrival","side":"United States"},{"name":"George S. Greene","role":"XII Corps division commander who crossed behind Hooker during the night","side":"United States"},{"name":"Sebastian Duncan","role":"13th New Jersey lieutenant whose diary recorded a prayer for courage on the night before battle","side":"United States"},{"name":"Robert E. Lee","role":"Army of Northern Virginia commander who responded to Hooker’s probe by reinforcing the Confederate left","side":"Confederacy"},{"name":"Thomas J. “Stonewall” Jackson","role":"Confederate wing commander ordered to shift his divisions behind Hood on the northern battlefield","side":"Confederacy"},{"name":"James Longstreet","role":"Confederate wing commander who sent Hood’s division north to confront Hooker","side":"Confederacy"},{"name":"John Bell Hood","role":"Confederate division commander whose brigades checked Meade’s advance in the East Woods","side":"Confederacy","image":"/images/ep189-john-bell-hood.jpg","source":"Library of Congress, Civil War Photograph Collection"},{"name":"J. E. B. Stuart","role":"Confederate cavalry commander who passed warning of Hooker’s crossing to Lee","side":"Confederacy"},{"name":"Ezra A. Carman","role":"Antietam veteran, historian, and Battlefield Board expert cited in the episode on the lack of reconnaissance before Hooker’s advance","side":"United States"}],"losses":[],"gallery":[{"image":"/images/ep189-mcclellan-battle-plan.svg","alt":"Schematic map of McClellan’s intended attack sequence and initial United States deployment at Antietam.","caption":"McClellan placed the opening weight on Hooker’s northern attack, gave Burnside an imprecise role against the Confederate right, and held a large center and reserve for reinforcement, defense, or a conditional attack.","source":"McClellan’s official reports, U.S. Army Center of Military History, Antietam order of battle, and episode transcript; see sources below."},{"image":"/images/ep189-hooker-september-16-advance.svg","alt":"Schematic map of Hooker’s September 16 crossing, southward turn, and encounter with Hood.","caption":"I Corps used the Upper Bridge and Pry’s Ford, moved west, and turned south toward Sharpsburg. Meade encountered Hood in the East Woods while Mansfield followed during the night.","source":"Hooker’s and Meade’s official reports, Antietam National Battlefield, Antietam Battlefield Board tablets, U.S. Army Center of Military History, and episode transcript; see sources below."},{"image":"/images/ep189-hooker-headquarters.jpg","alt":"Alexander Gardner photograph of the Poffenberger farm landscape identified as Hooker’s headquarters at Antietam.","caption":"Gardner’s September 1862 photograph records the farm landscape identified as Hooker’s Antietam headquarters. I Corps camped around the Poffenberger farm after its September 16 advance.","source":"Library of Congress, Civil War Glass Negatives and Related Prints; see sources below."},{"image":"/images/ep189-joseph-hooker.jpg","alt":"Brady-Handy glass-plate portrait of Major General Joseph Hooker in United States Army uniform.","caption":"Hooker had received command of I Corps on September 6, only eleven days before Antietam. The assignment on Lee’s left was his first critical independent corps operation.","source":"Library of Congress, Brady-Handy photograph collection; see sources below."},{"image":"/images/ep189-john-bell-hood.jpg","alt":"Library of Congress portrait of Confederate Brigadier General John Bell Hood.","caption":"Hood’s Texas and Law brigades moved north from reserve near the Dunker Church and met Meade’s leading division near the East Woods as daylight failed.","source":"Library of Congress, Civil War Photograph Collection; see sources below."}],"sources":[{"label":"Official Civil War Podcast episode 189 page","url":"https://civilwarpodcast.org/2017/04/02/civil-war-podcast-episode-189/"},{"label":"National Park Service: Antietam battle detail","url":"https://www.nps.gov/civilwar/search-battles-detail.htm?battleCode=MD003"},{"label":"Antietam National Battlefield: Antietam lead-up and aftermath","url":"https://www.nps.gov/articles/antietam-lead-up-to-and-after.htm"},{"label":"Antietam National Battlefield: The North Woods","url":"https://www.nps.gov/places/the-north-woods.htm"},{"label":"Antietam National Battlefield: The East Woods","url":"https://www.nps.gov/places/the-east-woods.htm"},{"label":"Antietam National Battlefield: The Cornfield","url":"https://www.nps.gov/places/the-cornfield.htm"},{"label":"Antietam National Battlefield: The Pry Farm","url":"https://www.nps.gov/places/the-pry-farm.htm"},{"label":"U.S. Army Center of Military History: The Maryland and Fredericksburg Campaigns, 1862–1863","url":"https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/GOVPUB-D114-PURL-gpo63555/pdf/GOVPUB-D114-PURL-gpo63555.pdf"},{"label":"U.S. Army Center of Military History: Antietam staff ride guide","url":"https://history.army.mil/portals/143/Images/Publications/catalog/35-3-1.pdf"},{"label":"U.S. Army Center of Military History: Antietam order of battle","url":"https://history.army.mil/portals/143/Images/Publications/catalog/75-6.pdf"},{"label":"Official Records, Series I, Volume XIX, Part I: Maryland Campaign reports","url":"https://ehistory.osu.edu/books/official-records/027"},{"label":"Primary source: George B. McClellan’s official reports","url":"https://antietam.aotw.org/exhibit.php?exhibit_id=19"},{"label":"Primary source: Joseph Hooker’s official reports","url":"https://antietam.aotw.org/exhibit.php?exhibit_id=246"},{"label":"Primary source: George G. Meade’s official report","url":"https://antietam.aotw.org/exhibit.php?exhibit_id=25"},{"label":"Primary source: John Bell Hood’s official report","url":"https://antietam.aotw.org/exhibit.php?exhibit_id=31"},{"label":"Antietam Battlefield Board: I Corps, September 16 tablet","url":"https://antietam.aotw.org/tablet.php?tablet_id=1190"},{"label":"Library of Congress: Gen. Joe Hooker’s headquarters at Antietam","url":"https://www.loc.gov/item/2018671472/"},{"label":"Library of Congress: Gen. Joe Hooker portrait","url":"https://www.loc.gov/item/2017895917/"},{"label":"Library of Congress: John Bell Hood portrait","url":"https://www.loc.gov/item/2002722867/"},{"label":"Official podcast book-recommendations list","url":"https://civilwarpodcast.org/book-recommendations/"}]}}]}]
13:["$","section",null,{"className":"reference-section maps-section","id":"maps","children":[["$","h2",null,{"children":"Maps"}],["$","div",null,{"className":"maps-grid","children":[["$","figure","Hooker’s headquarters on the northern battlefield",{"className":"reference-map","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"map-frame static-map-frame","children":["$","a",null,{"className":"map-image-link","href":"/projects/civil-war-field-companion/images/ep189-hooker-headquarters.jpg","target":"_blank","rel":"noreferrer","aria-label":"Open Hooker’s headquarters on the northern battlefield at full size","children":["$","img",null,{"src":"/projects/civil-war-field-companion/images/ep189-hooker-headquarters.jpg","alt":"Alexander Gardner photograph of the Poffenberger farm landscape identified as General Joseph Hooker’s headquarters at Antietam.","width":1600,"height":1200,"loading":"lazy"}]}]}],["$","figcaption",null,{"children":[["$","strong",null,{"children":"Hooker’s headquarters on the northern battlefield"}],["$","span",null,{"children":"Alexander Gardner photographed the house identified by the Library of Congress as Hooker’s headquarters at Antietam. National Park Service interpretation places Hooker’s September 16 headquarters in the Poffenberger barn; the photograph records the farm landscape where I Corps bivouacked before its dawn attack."}],["$","span",null,{"className":"map-actions","children":[["$","a",null,{"href":"/projects/civil-war-field-companion/images/ep189-hooker-headquarters.jpg","target":"_blank","rel":"noreferrer","children":["Open full-size map ",["$","$Le",null,{"ref":"$undefined","iconNode":"$d:props:children:1:props:children:0:props:children:1:props:children:2:props:iconNode","className":"lucide-arrow-up-right","size":13}]]}],["$","small",null,{"children":"Library of Congress Prints and Photographs Division and Antietam National Battlefield; see sources below."}]]}],"$undefined"]}]]}]]}]]}]
14:["$","section",null,{"className":"reference-section","id":"people","children":[["$","h2",null,{"children":"People and command"}],[[["$","link","css:/projects/civil-war-field-companion/_next/static/css/page.m4AAmS8w.css",{"rel":"stylesheet","precedence":"vite-rsc/importer-resources","href":"/projects/civil-war-field-companion/_next/static/css/page.m4AAmS8w.css","data-rsc-css-href":"/projects/civil-war-field-companion/_next/static/css/page.m4AAmS8w.css"}],["$","link","css:/projects/civil-war-field-companion/_next/static/css/index.B0pjVDS7.css",{"rel":"stylesheet","precedence":"vite-rsc/importer-resources","href":"/projects/civil-war-field-companion/_next/static/css/index.B0pjVDS7.css","data-rsc-css-href":"/projects/civil-war-field-companion/_next/static/css/index.B0pjVDS7.css"}],["$","link","css:/projects/civil-war-field-companion/_next/static/css/command-trees.KCNQiAeH.css",{"rel":"stylesheet","precedence":"vite-rsc/importer-resources","href":"/projects/civil-war-field-companion/_next/static/css/command-trees.KCNQiAeH.css","data-rsc-css-href":"/projects/civil-war-field-companion/_next/static/css/command-trees.KCNQiAeH.css"}],"$undefined"],[["$","div",null,{"className":"command-grid reference-command","children":[["$","section","United States",{"className":"command-column #315f8c","children":[["$","h3",null,{"children":"United States"}],[["$","div","Army of the Potomac-Maj. Gen. George B. McClellan",{"className":"command-entry","children":[["$","strong",null,{"children":"Army of the Potomac"}],["$","span",null,{"children":"Maj. Gen. George B. McClellan"}],["$","small",null,{"children":"Directed the army from the Pry farm; assigned the opening attack to Hooker, an attack against the Confederate right to Burnside, and retained the center and reserve under his immediate control"}]]}],["$","div","I Corps and northern sector-Maj. Gen. Joseph Hooker",{"className":"command-entry","children":[["$","strong",null,{"children":"I Corps and northern sector"}],["$","span",null,{"children":"Maj. Gen. Joseph Hooker"}],["$","small",null,{"children":"Led the opening movement with the divisions of Abner Doubleday, James B. Ricketts, and George G. Meade; Mansfield’s XII Corps was ordered to support him"}]]}],["$","div","Pennsylvania Reserves Division, I Corps-Brig. Gen. George G. Meade",{"className":"command-entry","children":[["$","strong",null,{"children":"Pennsylvania Reserves Division, I Corps"}],["$","span",null,{"children":"Brig. Gen. George G. Meade"}],["$","small",null,{"children":"Crossed the Upper Bridge in the lead and made the principal evening contact with Hood near the East Woods"}]]}],["$","div","2nd Division, I Corps-Brig. Gen. James B. Ricketts",{"className":"command-entry","children":[["$","strong",null,{"children":"2nd Division, I Corps"}],["$","span",null,{"children":"Brig. Gen. James B. Ricketts"}],["$","small",null,{"children":"Crossed at the Upper Bridge and formed on Hooker’s left in supporting distance"}]]}],["$","div","1st Division, I Corps-Brig. Gen. Abner Doubleday",{"className":"command-entry","children":[["$","strong",null,{"children":"1st Division, I Corps"}],["$","span",null,{"children":"Brig. Gen. Abner Doubleday"}],["$","small",null,{"children":"Crossed at Pry’s Ford and formed on Hooker’s right as the corps turned south"}]]}],["$","div","XII Corps-Maj. Gen. Joseph K. F. Mansfield",{"className":"command-entry","children":[["$","strong",null,{"children":"XII Corps"}],["$","span",null,{"children":"Maj. Gen. Joseph K. F. Mansfield"}],["$","small",null,{"children":"Led Alpheus S. Williams’s and George S. Greene’s divisions across the upper Antietam late on September 16 to support Hooker"}]]}],["$","div","II Corps-Maj. Gen. Edwin V. Sumner",{"className":"command-entry","children":[["$","strong",null,{"children":"II Corps"}],["$","span",null,{"children":"Maj. Gen. Edwin V. Sumner"}],["$","small",null,{"children":"Commanded the largest United States corps, held in the center east of Antietam Creek until ordered into battle the next morning"}]]}],"$L1f","$L20","$L21","$L22"]]}],"$L23"]}],"$L24"]]]}]
15:["$","section",null,{"className":"reference-section","id":"images","children":[["$","h2",null,{"children":"Historical images"}],["$","div",null,{"className":"history-gallery","children":[["$","$L25","/images/ep189-hooker-headquarters.jpg",{"image":"/images/ep189-hooker-headquarters.jpg","alt":"Alexander Gardner photograph of the Poffenberger farm landscape identified as Hooker’s headquarters at Antietam.","caption":"Gardner’s September 1862 photograph records the farm landscape identified as Hooker’s Antietam headquarters. I Corps camped around the Poffenberger farm after its September 16 advance.","name":"Alexander Gardner photograph of the Poffenberger farm landscape identified as Hooker’s headquarters at Antietam.","source":"$undefined","credit":"Library of Congress, Civil War Glass Negatives and Related Prints; see sources below."}],["$","$L25","/images/ep189-joseph-hooker.jpg",{"image":"/images/ep189-joseph-hooker.jpg","alt":"Brady-Handy glass-plate portrait of Major General Joseph Hooker in United States Army uniform.","caption":"Hooker had received command of I Corps on September 6, only eleven days before Antietam. The assignment on Lee’s left was his first critical independent corps operation.","name":"Brady-Handy glass-plate portrait of Major General Joseph Hooker in United States Army uniform.","source":"$undefined","credit":"Library of Congress, Brady-Handy photograph collection; see sources below."}],["$","$L25","/images/ep189-john-bell-hood.jpg",{"image":"/images/ep189-john-bell-hood.jpg","alt":"Library of Congress portrait of Confederate Brigadier General John Bell Hood.","caption":"Hood’s Texas and Law brigades moved north from reserve near the Dunker Church and met Meade’s leading division near the East Woods as daylight failed.","name":"Library of Congress portrait of Confederate Brigadier General John Bell Hood.","source":"$undefined","credit":"Library of Congress, Civil War Photograph Collection; see sources below."}]]}]]}]
